# ============================== HR 7 ==============================
LESSONS_H7 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Lifecycle events: scope and record structure", "11", "<p>This module covers the employment lifecycle events between joining and exit in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: execute transfers through the Employee Transfer document; execute promotions through the Employee Promotion document with the pay boundary operated; record and resolve grievances through the Employee Grievance document; run separations through the Employee Separation document and its template; conduct the Exit Interview; and operate probation confirmations and contract renewals on their standing reads.</p><p><b>The record structure.</b> Lifecycle events use five record types:</p><p>1. <b>Employee Transfer</b> — the document that moves an employee between branches, departments, or companies, applying the property changes from its date (Chapter 2).<br>2. <b>Employee Promotion</b> — the document that changes designation and related properties from its date (Chapter 3).<br>3. <b>Employee Grievance</b> — the record of a raised grievance, its investigation, and its resolution (Chapter 4).<br>4. <b>Employee Separation</b> — the exit's task checklist, generated from a template like onboarding (Chapter 5).<br>5. <b>Exit Interview</b> — the structured exit conversation's record (Chapter 5).</p><p><b>The design principle, carried from module 1.</b> Every lifecycle event is a change to the Employee master executed as a document: the document carries the decision, the date, and the property changes; the master's history shows what changed, when, and on whose authority. The alternative — editing Employee fields directly when someone moves — leaves the same fields changed with no document stating why, and the standing rule applies: no change without its record.</p><p><b>The boundaries with other tracks, stated once.</b></p><p>1. <b>Pay:</b> a transfer or promotion that changes pay executes the pay change through the Payroll track's lanes (the approval matrix, the letter, the Salary Structure Assignment — its module 6). The lifecycle document applies the organisational properties; it does not carry the pay change.<br>2. <b>The exit:</b> the Payroll track's module 7 owns the exit's money — the final slip, encashment, the settlement, the release checklist. This module owns the exit's HR side — the separation checklist, the exit interview, the record closure — and the two run together: the separation template's activities include the payroll lines, and both meet at the two-field rule (Relieving Date, Status).<br>3. <b>Access:</b> the sysadmin's joiner/mover/leaver procedures execute the access side of every event here; the checklists carry the confirmation lines.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the Employee Transfer. Chapter 3: the Employee Promotion. Chapter 4: the Employee Grievance. Chapter 5: the Employee Separation and the Exit Interview. Chapter 6: probation confirmations and contract renewals. Chapter 7: the lifecycle reads. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Employee Transfer", "12", "<p>Open Employee Transfer (awesome bar) &gt; New. One document per move. Complete the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Employee.</b> Select the employee being transferred.</p><p><b>2. Transfer Date.</b> The effective date, from the documented decision (the transfer memo, the approved request). The properties apply from this date; the date is the decision's, entered exactly — the honest-date rule from the payroll track applies to organisational changes identically.</p><p><b>3. The transfer details table.</b> One row per Employee property the transfer changes:</p><p>• <b>Property</b> — the field being changed: Branch, Department, Designation (where the move changes role), Reports To, Holiday List (where the destination's working pattern differs), Payroll Cost Center (where cost addressing is per employee — the branch move that forgets this row posts the salary to the old branch's P&amp;L from the transfer date).<br>• <b>Current</b> — pre-filled from the record.<br>• <b>New</b> — the destination value, from its master.</p><p>Build the row set from a standing transfer checklist of properties, not from memory — the table's completeness is the transfer's quality: each property forgotten is a report, a routing, or a posting that stays wrong until someone notices.</p><p><b>4. Inter-company transfers.</b> Where the move crosses legal entities, the transfer handles the company change per the site's configuration (including the new-employee-record option where the destination company requires its own record — the Payroll track, module 8's rule: employment is per company). An inter-company move is an exit-and-join in legal substance: the payroll and statutory consequences run with the accountant (final computations in the origin company per its rules, fresh assignment in the destination), and the transfer document coordinates the organisational side.</p><p><b>5. Submit.</b> On submission the property changes apply to the Employee record from the transfer date. Verify after submission: the Employee record's changed fields, the Organizational Chart where Reports To moved, and the destination's approver routing picking the employee up (module 1, Chapter 7's designs).</p><p><b>The transfer procedure.</b></p><p>1. The decision documented and approved per the business's design; attached to the transfer.<br>2. The property rows built from the checklist; the pay question answered explicitly — pay changes, if any, routed to the payroll lanes with the transfer referenced.<br>3. The document submitted effective the honest date.<br>4. The cross-track lines confirmed: access changes per the sysadmin mover procedure (role/branch scoping); compliance obligations triggered by the destination population (module 6, Chapter 7 — the stores-to-dispensing move's training line); the destination Holiday List and shift arrangements (module 3) in place before the first working day.<br>5. The verification reads run (step 5 above) and the transfer noted on the movement record (Chapter 7).</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Employee Promotion", "12", "<p>Open Employee Promotion (awesome bar) &gt; New. One document per promotion. The Payroll track's module 6 covered this document from the pay side; this chapter is the HR execution.</p><p><b>1. Employee and Promotion Date.</b> The employee and the effective date from the approved decision — the promotion letter is the anchor document (the payroll track's letter rule), and the date here is the letter's date.</p><p><b>2. The promotion details table.</b> One row per property the promotion changes:</p><p>• <b>Designation</b> — the new title, from the Designation master (the promotion's defining row).<br>• <b>Grade</b> — where grades are in use, the new grade (and the payroll consequence of a grade's Default Salary Structure is verified against the letter at the assignment — never assumed from the default).<br>• <b>Department / Reports To</b> — where the promotion changes the reporting position (the new supervisor's own reports re-pointed per module 1, Chapter 7 where the promotion creates a manager).</p><p><b>3. The silent-difference check.</b> The payroll track's finding, restated as the HR-side procedure: when the promotion moves the employee between salary structures (a different package family), the two structures' component lists are compared line by line before the letter finalises — a component present in one and absent in the other (the meal allowance absent from the Management structure) is a difference the letter must state, or payday delivers it as a surprise. The comparison is the promotion checklist's line, run with the payroll operator.</p><p><b>4. Submit.</b> The property changes apply from the promotion date. The pay change — where the promotion carries one — travels the payroll lanes in full: the approval on the matrix, the letter, the Salary Structure Assignment from the same effective date. The promotion document and the assignment reference each other; the payroll track's quarterly naked-assignment sweep verifies the pairing from its side, and the movement record (Chapter 7) carries the event from this side.</p><p><b>The promotion procedure.</b></p><p>1. The case assembled: the appraisal history (module 5's records — the readiness evidence), the role's requirements (the Designation description), the training record (module 6).<br>2. The decision approved per the matrix; the letter issued stating title, effective date, pay terms, and any structure differences (step 3's check).<br>3. The Employee Promotion document entered and submitted effective the letter's date; the assignment entered by the payroll operator from the same letter.<br>4. The cross-track lines: access changes where the new role carries them (the sysadmin mover procedure — the new manager's approver roles); approver-table updates where the promotion makes the employee an approver (module 1's department tables); compliance obligations of the new population where any.<br>5. Verification: the Employee record's properties, the Organizational Chart, the first slip after the effective date read against the letter (the payroll operator's line, confirmed jointly).</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The Employee Grievance", "12", "<p>Open Employee Grievance (awesome bar) &gt; New. One record per raised grievance. The record's discipline is the chapter's substance: grievances handled off the record produce disputes with no facts.</p><p><b>1. The record's fields.</b></p><p>• <b>Raised By / Subject</b> — the employee raising it and the grievance's subject line.<br>• <b>Grievance Type</b> — from the type master (create the categories per the written grievance policy: working conditions, conduct, pay dispute, harassment, other). The type routes and reports; a harassment type in particular carries the policy's specific handling requirements.<br>• <b>Grievance Against</b> — the party the grievance concerns, per the form's structure.<br>• <b>Date</b>, <b>Description</b>, and <b>Cause</b> — the account as raised, entered as stated: the record holds the raiser's account in the raiser's terms, and the investigation's findings are entered separately (below), not blended into the original account.<br>• <b>Status</b> — Open → Investigated → Resolved (or Invalid per the policy's finding).<br>• <b>Resolution fields</b> — the resolution detail, the resolved-by, and the date.</p><p><b>2. The handling procedure, per the written grievance policy.</b></p><p>1. <b>Receipt:</b> the grievance entered on the day it is raised (by the employee through self-service where enabled, or by HR on the employee's behalf with the authority noted — the no-proxy rule from module 4 applies in kind). The raiser receives the acknowledgment the policy promises, with the timeline.<br>2. <b>Routing:</b> per type and per the policy — the line manager for working-conditions matters, HR and the owner for conduct matters, the policy's named route for harassment (which never routes through the person complained about — the routing design is checked for this before the first grievance, not during it).<br>3. <b>Investigation:</b> the investigator gathers the facts and enters findings in the resolution section as observations with their sources — the recruitment module's rule (observations, not speculation) applies with full weight here. Pay-dispute grievances route through the payroll query procedure (its module 4) for the arithmetic and return here for any policy matter the arithmetic exposes.<br>4. <b>Resolution:</b> the decision per the policy's authority, entered with its reasoning; the status moved; the raiser informed per the policy's communication line.<br>5. <b>Confidentiality:</b> grievance records are among the most sensitive records in the system — the access matrix restricts them to the handling roles, and the module 1 sensitive-data rules apply at their strictest.</p><p><b>3. The grievance reads.</b> Quarterly, to the owner, as figures: grievances by type, by status age (an Open grievance aging past the policy's timeline is a process failure regardless of its merits), and by unit. The read is data per the standing rule — a unit's grievance cluster is the owner's signal to look, and the records give the look its facts.</p><p><b>4. What the record protects.</b> Both parties. The raiser has a dated record that the matter was raised and what was done; the business has a dated record of its handling; the person complained about has findings entered as observations rather than accumulated impressions. The employment disputes that reach lawyers are argued from exactly these records — or from their absence.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The Employee Separation and the Exit Interview", "13", "<p>The exit's HR machinery: the separation checklist and the structured exit conversation. The Payroll track's module 7 runs the money; this chapter runs beside it.</p><p><b>1. The Employee Separation Template.</b> Open Employee Separation Template (awesome bar) &gt; New — built once, like the onboarding template (module 2, Chapter 7), with one row per exit activity:</p><p>• The activity, the responsible user or role, and the timing relative to the relieving date.<br>• Standard rows: the two-field entry — Relieving Date and Status — on the decision's day (HR); the leave balance confirmation with the employee at the exit's start (HR — the payroll track's encashment reads it); the handover of duties documented (the manager); the direct reports re-pointed where the leaver manages (HR, module 1's rule); the exit interview scheduled and held (HR — below); company property collected against the custody list (Administration); access closure on the relieving date (the sysadmin's leaver procedure — confirmed, not assumed); the payroll settlement lines (the payroll operator — the settlement sheet, the statutory tail, the release checklist per that track); the record closure — Reason for Leaving, exit fields, attachments complete (HR).</p><p><b>2. The Employee Separation document.</b> Open Employee Separation &gt; New at exit confirmation: the employee, the relieving date, the template. Submission generates the activity tasks to their owners with dates — the exit as a visible checklist across four functions instead of a corridor coordination. The boarding status tracks completion; the exit closes when every activity does, and the payroll track's release checklist and this document confirm each other's lines at the end.</p><p><b>3. The Exit Interview.</b> Open Exit Interview (awesome bar) &gt; New — the structured exit conversation's record:</p><p>• The employee (the relieving date and details pulled from the record), the interviewer (per the policy — HR or a manager not in the leaver's line, so the conversation can be candid), the scheduled date, and the status.<br>• The interview runs on the policy's standing question set — the reasons for leaving in the leaver's terms, the experience of the role and management, what the business should know — and the responses are recorded as given (the as-stated rule from Chapter 4).<br>• Held before the last day where possible (the last-afternoon interview competes with goodbyes and loses), and never made a condition of the settlement — the payroll track's release checklist does not include it, deliberately: the interview is the business asking a favour of someone leaving, and its honesty depends on that being true.</p><p><b>4. Reading the exits.</b> Two instruments, quarterly and annually:</p><p>1. <b>The attrition read</b> — leavers by Reason for Leaving (module 1's field), by unit, by tenure band: the turnover picture as figures. A unit whose leavers cluster in the first year is an onboarding or management signal; a reason category rising across units is a policy signal. Data to the owner per the standing rule.<br>2. <b>The exit-interview themes</b> — the interviews read together for recurring themes, summarised without attribution per the policy's confidentiality line. The themes join the attrition figures in the annual review: the numbers say who leaves; the interviews say why in their own words; the two together are the retention conversation's facts.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Probation confirmations and contract renewals", "12", "<p>Two scheduled lifecycle events that fail silently when untracked. Both run on standing reads from module 1's fields.</p><p><b>1. Probation confirmations.</b></p><p>1. <b>The read:</b> monthly, the Employee list filtered on probation-period fields — probation end dates falling in the next sixty days, by manager. The list goes to the managers with the confirmation procedure attached.<br>2. <b>The assessment:</b> the probation review runs as a small appraisal cycle (module 5, Chapter 7's rule): the probation template, the manager's assessment on the record, the decision informed by the score.<br>3. <b>The outcomes, each as a record:</b> <b>confirmed</b> — the confirmation letter issued, the confirmation date field completed on the Employee record, any terms that change at confirmation (the policy's probation terms) executed through their lanes; <b>extended</b> — where the policy allows extension: the letter stating the extension and its reasons, the field updated, the next review scheduled; <b>not confirmed</b> — the exit per the probation terms, through the full separation machinery (Chapter 5 and the payroll track's module 7 — a probation exit is an exit, with every checklist line).<br>4. <b>The failure mode named:</b> the probation that lapses into confirmation by silence — the end date passing with no assessment, the employee confirmed by default with no record. The monthly read exists to make the silent lapse impossible: every end date gets a decision, dated, on the record.</p><p><b>2. Contract renewals.</b></p><p>1. <b>The read:</b> monthly, the expiring-contracts report (module 1, Chapter 3's contract end date field) — contracts ending in the next ninety days, by unit. Ninety days because the decision needs time on both sides: the business deciding, the employee planning.<br>2. <b>The decision, each as a record:</b> <b>renewed</b> — the new contract signed before the old expires; the contract end date updated from the new document; the terms changes, if any, through their lanes; <b>converted</b> — the fixed-term becoming permanent per the decision: the letter, the Employment Type updated (module 1's master), the policy consequences (leave policy differences by type — module 4) executed from the conversion date; <b>ended</b> — the contract expiring per its terms: the exit through the separation machinery, scheduled from the known date (the most schedulable exit there is — the payroll track's retirement observation applies: known dates make clean exits).<br>3. <b>The failure mode named:</b> the contract that expires unnoticed — the employee working on, the legal position unclear, the eventual regularisation done backwards. The blank end date on a fixed-term record (module 1's warning) and the skipped monthly read are the two causes; the register hygiene filter and the standing read are the two preventions.</p><p><b>3. The shared pattern.</b> Both events are date arithmetic on maintained fields plus a decision lane. The pattern repeats across the programme — the compliance expiries (module 6), the payroll year lines, the holiday list build — and it is the module's demonstration case for Chapter 9's rule: scheduled events are read from the records on a calendar, decided on time, and executed as documents.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The lifecycle reads and the movement record", "12", "<p>The standing instruments across all lifecycle events.</p><p><b>1. The movement record.</b> The payroll track (module 6) keeps the movement record for pay events; the HR side extends it to all lifecycle events — one register, one line per event:</p><p>• The employee, the event type (transfer, promotion, confirmation, extension, renewal, conversion, separation), the effective date, the document reference, and the decision reference.<br>• Maintained as events execute (the procedure chapters each end by noting the event here), and read quarterly against the documents: every line has its document; every lifecycle document has its line. The reconciliation is the same both-ways read as everywhere else in the programme.</p><p><b>2. The quarterly lifecycle read, to the owner.</b> One page of figures:</p><p>1. Events by type for the quarter (the movement record's counts).<br>2. The attrition figures (Chapter 5's read).<br>3. The scheduled-event health: probation decisions made versus due; contract decisions made versus due; any lapse listed with its cause (Chapter 6's failure modes, counted).<br>4. Open grievances by age against the policy timeline (Chapter 4).</p><p><b>3. The cross-track confirmations, gathered.</b> Every lifecycle event touches other tracks, and each procedure chapter carried confirmation lines. Gathered here as the standing checklist rows any event's document should show completed:</p><p>1. <b>Access</b> — the sysadmin's joiner/mover/leaver procedure line, confirmed on the event's checklist (the transfer's scoping change, the promotion's new roles, the separation's same-day closure).<br>2. <b>Pay</b> — where the event carries a pay consequence: the payroll lanes' documents referenced from the lifecycle document (the assignment, the letter).<br>3. <b>Compliance</b> — population changes triggering training obligations (module 6's mover rule).<br>4. <b>Routing</b> — approver tables and Reports To updated where the event changes them (module 1).</p><p><b>4. The history discipline, restated for lifecycle.</b> Lifecycle documents apply changes forward from honest dates; the Employee record's history (track changes per the standing audit configuration) shows the sequence. Three rules:</p><p>1. Backdating is the honest-date lane only: an event executed late is entered with its true effective date and processed with its true entry date — the arrears pattern from the payroll track, applied to organisational changes (the transfer applied a month late corrects reports and routing forward, and the gap is noted, not hidden).<br>2. Documents are never cancelled to rewrite outcomes: a wrong transfer is corrected by a correcting transfer with its cause noted.<br>3. The Employee record's Version history plus the movement record together answer any question of the form <i>when did this person's role change and on whose authority</i> — the answer is two lookups, which is the standard the whole module builds to.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: a HealthTrade quarter of lifecycle events", "13", "<p>This chapter applies Chapters 2-7 to one HealthTrade quarter. Configuration values and outcomes are listed per event.</p><p><b>Event 1 — a transfer (Chapter 2).</b> A warehouse assistant moves from Central Lagos to the Akure branch on the operations manager's approved memo. Employee Transfer entered: transfer date the 1st; property rows — Branch (Central Lagos → Akure), Department (unchanged), Reports To (the Akure branch manager), Holiday List (Standard → Retail Branches — Akure works Saturdays), Payroll Cost Center (Central Lagos → Akure). Submitted; verification: the record's fields, the chart, and the first post-transfer slip's cost center confirmed with the payroll operator. The compliance line fires: Akure's role includes dispatch handling — the Cold Chain obligation triggers from the move date and the employee joins the Q2 event's table (module 6's mover rule executed). Movement record: one line, memo referenced.</p><p><b>Event 2 — a promotion (Chapter 3).</b> A senior pharmacy technician is promoted to Pharmacy Counter supervisor. The case: two appraisal cycles' records, the training record showing the supervision programme completed. The silent-difference check runs with the payroll operator: the destination structure carries a Responsibility Allowance absent from the origin — stated in the letter. Employee Promotion entered effective the letter's date: Designation and Reports To rows; the counter staff re-pointed to the new supervisor; the department approver table updated (the new supervisor joins the leave approvers). The assignment enters from the same letter; the first slip is read jointly against it. Movement record: one line, letter referenced.</p><p><b>Event 3 — a grievance (Chapter 4).</b> A driver raises a working-conditions grievance (vehicle safety) through self-service. Entered as stated, type Working Conditions, routed to the operations manager per the policy. Investigation findings entered as observations: two of the raised items verified (a worn tyre set, a faulty jack), one not reproduced. Resolution: the verified items fixed with dates, the resolution recorded, the raiser informed; status Resolved in eleven days against the policy's fourteen-day timeline. The quarterly read carries it as one line.</p><p><b>Event 4 — a separation (Chapter 5).</b> An accounts assistant resigns with notice (the payroll track's module 7 worked example is this exit's money side; this is the HR side). Employee Separation generated from the template: nine activities across HR, the manager, Administration, the sysadmin, and the payroll operator. The Exit Interview is held four days before the last day by the HR lead (not the line manager); responses recorded as given. All activities close by the relieving date; the separation's checklist and the payroll release checklist confirm each other's lines. The attrition read gains one line: resignation, Finance, tenure three years.</p><p><b>Event 5 — the scheduled reads (Chapter 6).</b> The monthly probation read lists one name — assessed as a small cycle, confirmed, the letter issued and the confirmation date field completed. The contract read lists two contracts ending inside ninety days: one renewed (new contract signed three weeks before expiry, end date updated), one converted to permanent (Employment Type updated, the leave policy difference executed from the conversion date per module 4).</p><p><b>The quarter's close.</b> The movement record shows seven lines, each with its document; the both-ways reconciliation is clean. The quarterly page to the MD: seven events by type, one resignation, probation and contract decisions two-for-two on time, zero lapses, one grievance resolved inside the timeline. Every figure traces to a document in two lookups.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in lifecycle administration, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the fields edited instead of the document.</b> Symptom: Branch, Designation, or Reports To changed directly on the Employee record; the change real but undocumented; the movement record silent. Correction: the event documented retrospectively — the lifecycle document entered with the honest dates and the late entry noted. Prevention: the rule that lifecycle changes travel through their documents, and the quarterly both-ways reconciliation catching direct edits.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the incomplete property table.</b> Symptom: the transfer that moved the branch but not the cost center, the holiday list, or the reporting line; the salary posting to the old branch, the leave counting the wrong calendar. Correction: the missing rows applied by a correcting document. Prevention: the standing property checklist at transfer entry — the table built from the list, not from memory.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the promotion pay surprise.</b> Symptom: the promoted employee's first slip differing from expectation; a component present in one structure and absent in the other, discovered on payday. Correction: the payroll query procedure, then the letter or structure corrected per the decision. Prevention: the silent-difference check — the two structures compared line by line with the payroll operator before the letter finalises.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the grievance off the record.</b> Symptom: complaints handled in conversations; no dated record of what was raised or done; the eventual dispute argued from recollections. Correction: the matter entered when it resurfaces, with its history as reconstructable. Prevention: the entry-on-the-day rule, the routing design checked in advance, and the quarterly age read.</p><p><b>Error 5 — the silent lapse.</b> Symptom: probations confirmed by default; contracts expired with the employee working on; the regularisation done backwards under pressure. Correction: the position regularised through the proper documents with honest dates and the gap noted. Prevention: the monthly probation and contract reads — date arithmetic on maintained fields, decided on time.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the separation as one person's memory.</b> Symptom: exits coordinated by corridor; the property unreturned, the access alive, the interview skipped, the reports orphaned — each discovered later. Correction: the open items closed through their owners. Prevention: the Employee Separation template — the exit as generated tasks with owners and dates, confirmed against the payroll release checklist.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the five lifecycle record types and the three track boundaries; execute a transfer with the complete property table and the cross-track lines; execute a promotion with the silent-difference check and the pay boundary; record, route, investigate, and resolve a grievance per the written policy with the as-stated and observations rules; run a separation from the template with the Exit Interview held on its rules; operate the probation and contract reads with every outcome as a record; and maintain the movement record with the quarterly reconciliation and owner's page.</p><p><b>Module rule 7: lifecycle events are documents — transfers and promotions applying their property rows from honest dates, grievances entered as stated and resolved on the record, separations run as generated checklists beside the payroll exit, and every scheduled event decided on time from the standing reads.</b> Module 8 closes the track: settings, the assembled controls, and the handover test.</p>"),
]

